## Demo checklist

1. Start with `npm install` and `npm run dev`. Use localhost or HTTPS for the webcam.
2. Place the laptop at hand height with both players in good light. Runner stands
   on the left of the mirrored preview; saboteur stands on the right.
3. Allow camera access. Wait for both previews to say **Hand detected · Ready**.
4. Click Start once to enable audio. Check volume and mute with the sound button or M.
5. Show runner swipes/jump, then saboteur aim/fist. Explain that a warning precedes
   each attack and cards replenish automatically.
6. Demonstrate one collision, the score/survival screen, and a replay.
7. Keep `?novision` ready as a keyboard fallback. If camera setup fails, the banner
   offers Retry camera and Keyboard mode. A browser permission denial may need
   to be changed in the browser's site settings before retrying.
8. Before presenting, record a short successful two-player run as a backup.

### Production preview

Run `npm run build`, then `npm run preview -- --base /jonandthebeanstalk/`. Open
`http://localhost:4173/jonandthebeanstalk/` (or add `?novision`). Check that the title screen,
initial cards, runner, sound toggle, and replay work under this deployment prefix.
The vision debug page is `/jonandthebeanstalk/vision.html`.

Merging to main triggers the GitHub Pages deployment workflow. A repo admin must
have selected **GitHub Actions** as the Pages source. Local production preview
checks do not confirm that the remote deployment is live.

Remaining manual checks: live two-player gestures, camera denial/retry on the demo
browser, audio volume/listening, and a successful run on the deployed HTTPS site.

Production smoke check: the built game loads and starts under /jonandthebeanstalk/ when preview uses the explicit base above. Default preview currently uses / and cannot serve the prefixed assets correctly. Camera failure now offers retry and keyboard-mode links.

## Storybook presentation and pause

The shell now presents Jon (runner, left) versus the Giant (saboteur, right),
with illustrated vines, clouds, parchment cards, and a woodland palette. Menus,
results, and the HUD use native HTML text rather than scaled canvas text for
clearer rendering. The runner and saboteur lane owners maintain gameplay mechanics.

During a run, press **Esc** or click **Pause**. Resume with Esc or the Resume
button. Pausing freezes the scene, physics, animations, and feedback timers.
A shared adjusted update clock excludes pause time from score, attack cooldowns,
and powerup expiry. Camera previews continue; both players' gameplay inputs are
ignored while paused. Two minimal cross-lane input guards implement this in the
runner and saboteur handlers; mark the PR `[cross-lane]`.

Validation includes repeated pause/resume, unchanged score and aim while paused,
blocked attack input, and no card catch-up after a long pause. Clock unit tests
cover frozen time and repeated pauses. Live gesture testing remains outstanding.

Fairy theme expansion (explicitly requested): Jon is now drawn as a wizard with
violet robes, a crescent hat, and crystal staff. Three scrolling leafy beanstalk
stems replace the road while retaining lane centers and collision geometry.
Pastel skies, twinkling lights, fairy silhouettes, flowers, mushrooms, and a distant
cloud castle carry the theme through menus and gameplay. Art is generated locally;
there are no new dependencies or external asset requests. These runner visual
changes are also cross-lane and must be called out in the PR.

The previously intermittent saboteur test now fixes its random draw to a barrier,
so it consistently measures cooldown behavior rather than double-wall multiplicity.
An additional test checks that paused scenes ignore saboteur input.

Latest validation: all 36 tests, formatting, typecheck, and production build passed.
Production browser checks at 800 × 600 verified the HTML menu/HUD, wizard and
beanstalk artwork, pause input blocking, replay, and pause after replay. Survival
time excluded a long pause; no browser warnings/errors were recorded. Both theme
and pause are committed. Live two-player webcam and audio listening remain manual.

Retro magic pass: stepped spellbook frames, arcade-style native HTML typography,
nearest-filtered wizard/stalk textures, luminous stalk runes, a pixel-spark trail,
and a jump sparkle burst. Jump animation rises more visibly without changing its
collision duration. The HUD and cards explain counters: jump or dodge hurdles,
drifters, and oil; dodge walls and mud. Card panels stay outside the playable lanes
with a full three-card hand. Browser checks covered jumping, movement, full hands,
and Esc pause/resume; all 36 tests, typecheck, formatting, and build passed.

Giant attack pass: each pending attack now summons an oversized reaching hand with
a purple sleeve, gold cuff, curled fingers, and an attack-colored spell. It releases
with an impact ring when the warning completes, then retracts. Animations use scene
tweens, so Esc freezes them; pending hands are removed on game over/shutdown.
The giant starts with two cards (was one), draws every 2000 ms (was 3000), and fires
at most every 650 ms (was 750). The 500 ms warning and three-card limit stay intact.
Tests cover opening-hand limits, cooldowns, release, cancellation, and paused input.
This is explicitly requested cross-lane saboteur work on `prahalad/shell`.

## Current giant mechanic (replaces the earlier card/deck tuning)

The giant previews one random obstacle, aims with J/K/L or hand position, and drops
it with Space or a fist. Each drop starts a fixed 4-second cooldown; the HUD counts
down and reveals a new random obstacle when ready. Number-key card selection is
removed. Double-wall is excluded from the random pool so one drop spawns exactly
one obstacle. The legacy deck utility is no longer used by gameplay.

The reach-to-release wind-up is 400 ms (previously 500 ms). Obstacles now appear at
the hand's fingertips (y=110), rather than entering from above the screen, making
the release immediate and slightly shortening the approach. Artwork replaces flat
blocks with rune-carved mossy stones, thorn logs, bubbling hex puddles, tangled
roots, and faceted drifting crystals. The hand holds a matching miniature of the
actual obstacle before releasing it with a grip-opening motion and impact ring.

The 4-second cooldown uses the paused play clock; paused inputs remain blocked.
These requested changes affect the saboteur lane and require a `[cross-lane]` PR.

## Natural hazards and fireball

Wild thorns, mossy rocks, and sticky sap now grow along the beanstalk without giant
input. The first appears after 5 seconds; later hazards appear roughly every
4.5 seconds. If an obstacle is still near the entrance, spawning retries after
600 ms rather than stacking another hazard there. Each natural spawn occupies
one lane. Thorns can be jumped, rocks must be dodged, and sap slows Jon unless
jumped or dodged. All timing follows the paused game clock and resets per run.

Fireball replaces Drifter in the giant's random pool. It travels straight down the
aimed lane, has an animated orange/yellow flame, and must be dodged. Drifting lane
motion and its old configuration were removed. The four-second giant cooldown
and one-obstacle-per-throw rule remain intact.

Additional feature ideas were requested for discussion only; no extra features
beyond natural hazards and the fireball replacement were implemented in this pass.

Validation: all 42 tests, formatting, typecheck, and production build passed.
An idle-giant browser run confirmed natural hazards cause collisions independently;
visual checks covered the fireball and all three natural hazard types with no
browser errors. No brainstormed future feature has been started.

## Jon and the Beanstalk: progression update

All player-facing menus, HUD, camera labels, pause text, and browser title now use
Jon. Fireballs continue to the bottom rather than being consumed on contact;
a handled-hit flag prevents repeat hits after a shield absorbs one. During the
short game-over transition, fireballs keep moving while other hazards freeze.

Speed rises by 8 px/s per second (was 6), capped to preserve one second of reaction
time from the giant's fingertip release point. Natural waves begin with one hazard,
then use two distinct lanes after 45 seconds, leaving a third lane open. The spawn
interval falls from 4.5 seconds toward a 1.8-second minimum over 90 seconds. The
opening grace period and entrance-stacking checks remain. Pause excludes elapsed
time from both speed and density progression.

Latest validation: all 46 tests, formatting, typecheck, and production build passed.
A browser integration check confirmed a fireball hits once and exits the bottom
instead of disappearing at contact. Production preview confirms the Jon branding.

Pre-push integration: rebased onto the latest team main. Preserved spring-based
movement, jump buffering, collision surface behavior, runner effects, vision
updates, and the collapsible camera dock while retaining the wizard/beanstalk
art and local game mechanics. Combined the duplicate deterministic RNG test setup.
The complete scripts/check.sh passed with 110 tests; browser checks covered the
camera dock, wizard rendering, movement, and jump with no console warnings/errors.

## Five tales, intro, and music

The game follows the five collectible-driven fairy tales. Find the current
stage’s treasure to advance; the last tale continues as an endless challenge.
The opening Jon–Giant conversation is authored locally, with Next/Enter and
Skip/Esc controls. Replays start directly in gameplay.

An original quiet melody and bass loop accompanies gameplay. Sound/M mutes music
and effects together; Esc pauses both. Music stops at game over, and scheduled
voices and timers are cleaned up on replay. Accepted movement, pickups, shield
blocks, giant casts, and stage transitions have sound effects.

The latest team runner artwork, five-stage progression, collectible safety gaps,
vision changes, giant aiming, buffered casts, and authoritative cooldown feedback
are integrated. The upper-right decorative castle has been removed. Gemini
integration and the separate timed chapter mode were removed by request.

## Claude's rescue and the hidden message

The giant has kidnapped Jon's companion Claude and locked him in a tower cell.
The opening introduces the rescue; each of the five tales reveals one letter from
its collectible, and Xen's final badge reveals the sixth. Uncollected letters are
question marks in both visible text and accessible labels. Stage labels, opening
art, and dialogue do not reveal the complete answer ahead of collection.

At the tower, all six recovered clues appear in order beside a textbox. The cell
opens only when the player enters their message (case-insensitive, with outer
whitespace ignored). Wrong answers allow retries. Esc cannot bypass the lock.
Correct entry animates the bars opening, plays Jon and Claude's reunion, and
shows the rescue victory screen. Timers and focus handlers are cleaned up on
scene shutdown. Reduced motion disables decorative motion and opens bars directly.

Permanent powers and giant FIFO progression retain the previous balance limits:
10% longer jumps, 18px pickup reach, 25% shorter slows, a 22s recharging ward,
and 15% extra score. Giant capacity grows from one to three every two completed
tales; refills take four seconds per obstacle, with a 1.7s minimum drop gap.
Three-lane lethal walls are deferred. Esc resume uses a cancellable 3-2-1 countdown.

Validation: formatting, typecheck, all 183 tests, and production build pass.
Browser checks cover wrong answers, Esc protection, case-insensitive correct
entry, animated unlock, reunion, and the victory screen.

Textbox keyboard fix: game shortcuts ignore editable fields and modifier shortcuts.
All 184 tests pass. Browser verification used individual H/A/C/K/T/X key presses
with installKeyboard enabled, then Enter to unlock Claude successfully.

## Continuous cinematics, live clue popups, and richer audio

The current presentation replaces the opening and reunion slides with continuous
20/21-second in-game animations. A single vector scene animates the camera, giant,
Jon, Claude's capture, tower, escape, and walk home. Timed subtitles accompany the
action. Esc/Skip finishes the scene; reduced-motion preferences stop decorative
movement. The final textbox still must unlock Claude before the ending can play.

Collectibles no longer pause gameplay or open dialogue scenes. A compact top
popup reveals only the collected letter and permanent reward for 4.5 seconds.
The HUD keeps score, current objective, hidden-letter tracker, relic count, brief
controls, and a compact FIFO obstacle queue. Browser review confirmed the update
clock continues during collection and the popup clears on game time.

The original soundtrack is now more prominent, with melody, bass, synthesized
kick/snare/hats, and gently increasing stage tempo. New cues cover footsteps,
aiming, appearing treasures, clue reveals, relic magic, and expired powerups, in
addition to jumps, hits, shields, giant attacks, and victory. Sound/M still mutes
everything, and pause stops the music. Scheduled voices and scene listeners are
cleaned up on restart. No audio downloads or external services are required.

## Magical stage score and distinct obstacle sounds

Each tale now has an original theme with celesta-like bells, harp echoes and warm
sustained harmony. Tempo rises monotonically across stages: 100, 109, 120, 133,
150 and 167 BPM (eighth-note scheduling). Later stages add arpeggio layers and a
light pulse. Stage changes blend the old/new themes and tempo over 2.4 seconds;
active notes finish instead of being cut off. Mute, pause and restart cleanup remain.

Walls, double walls, hurdles, oil, mud, fireballs, thorns, rocks and sap each have a
unique synthesized signature, played when the obstacle actually enters the world
and again on unprotected contact. A short high-frequency glass shatter marks an
actual shield/ward break; the brief immunity afterward does not repeat it.

Cinematics reuse the existing 16-frame Jon run-left/run-right sheets and jump PNG,
respecting the deployed base path and reduced-motion preference. Claude and the
giant retain drawn artwork because no sprite sheets for them exist in the repo.
The ending dialogue and result text are now shorter and more restrained.

All 189 tests and full checks pass, including progressive tempo, unique obstacle
sounds, short shatter duration, and theme changes without cutting notes or adding
extra schedulers.

## Persistent aim lane and volume control

The red aiming lane now uses one alpha channel, with an opaque fill and a 22%
idle opacity. Previously the cast tween multiplied object alpha by fill alpha,
reducing visibility after the first drop. It remains visible through cooldowns,
repeated casts and stage changes.

Sound On/Off is replaced by a 0–100% volume slider. Drag it, use arrow keys, or
scroll over it in 5% increments. M mutes/restores the last chosen level. All music
and effects share the same master gain. Cinematic audio cleanup is idempotent so
an old scene cannot stop the next scene's music a second time. A live browser
analyser confirmed nonzero output after intro completion and theme change, and
zero output at 0% volume. The production preview is rebuilt under /hacktx2026/.

## Giant counterplay balance pass

Giant obstacles now release at y=220 instead of y=110, with the hand, held object,
warning and impact ring aligned to that position. Track speed stays capped at
440px/s. Against the largest lethal obstacle, the 400ms telegraph plus travel
still gives 1.15 seconds at top speed. The escape-lane gate also accounts for the
lower release point so mixed waves cannot close every unjumpable lane.

Pickup shields and the permanent ward now share one charge. A block consumes any
pickup shield and restarts the ward's 35-second recharge, preventing a second
block immediately afterward. Post-block immunity is 400ms instead of 800ms.
Mud/sap slow lateral responsiveness to 65% for their normal duration, with the
Cinderella reduction retained; they no longer slow the road. The separate slowmo
pickup still intentionally slows the world.

Giant stock refills every three seconds (was four), retaining the 1.7-second
minimum gap and growing FIFO capacity. Only the next attack is revealed in both
HUDs. Permanent jump/reach/score bonuses remain. All 222 tests and full checks
pass, including shared-charge consumption, near-ready ward prevention, slow
movement, refill timing, drop reaction budget, and escape-lane protection.

## Local single-player with live Gemini tactics

Choose Two players, Play as Jon (AI Giant), or Play as Giant (AI Jon) from the
simplified title screen. Single-player exposes Easy / Normal / Hard. The chosen
mode survives replay, and Change mode returns to the selector. Only the human's
side accepts keyboard/camera inputs; practice still teaches both sides. Giant
matches report catching Jon as a win and reaching the tower as a loss, without
asking the giant player to solve the rescue textbox.

Gemini supplies a validated tactical plan about every ten seconds (tracking,
prediction/ambush, preferred lane, aggression and jump preference). A local
controller executes normal legal inputs, never frame-by-frame API calls. It uses
delayed observations: Easy 480ms/18% mistakes, Normal 300ms/8%, Hard 180ms/2.5%.
Normal game cooldowns, obstacle placement, physics and shield rules still apply.
AI Jon avoids hazards and seeks treasures. AI Giant aims and fires through the
existing queue. Pause, game over and shutdown stop actions and abort requests.
Slow/unavailable Gemini requests keep a local opponent running; the badge shows
whether current tactics came from Gemini or are offline.

Run with Node 22.18+ (Node 24 also works):

1. Put GEMINI_API_KEY in ignored .env. GEMINI_MODEL defaults to
   gemini-3.5-flash-lite. Never use a VITE_ variable for the key.
2. Run npm run ai:server, then npm run dev. Vite proxies /api/ai to the loopback
   server on port 8787, including the production preview. The local server binds
   to 127.0.0.1 and permits the normal local dev/preview origins.
3. For deployment, GitHub Pages cannot run the server. Host the endpoint separately
   behind appropriate access controls, configure its allowed origins, and set the
   public VITE_AI_ENDPOINT URL before building. Until then, the static build uses
   local fallback opponents. The backend includes bounded payload validation,
   limited concurrency, per-session/global rate limits and request timeouts.

Only numeric game observations are sent to Gemini, never camera frames or the
puzzle answer. The API key remains on the server. Structured responses are locally
validated before use. References: https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/generate-content/structured-output

